Sogen: the null device takes handle flags and file information

Sogen's console handles and its null device are pseudo handles, the
same in every process. SetHandleInformation on one answered
STATUS_INVALID_HANDLE, as did NtSetInformationFile on the null device;
on Windows both succeed. A program that opens NUL for a child's stdin
marks the handle inheritable first and may set file information on it;
both now succeed, the flags kept nowhere since every process, a child
included, has the same handle.

SBCL's run-program opens NUL for a child with no input and marks it
inheritable; under Sogen it failed there, before any child started.

diff --git a/src/windows-emulator/syscalls/file.cpp b/src/windows-emulator/syscalls/file.cpp
index 78d4b93..b0350bf 100644
--- a/src/windows-emulator/syscalls/file.cpp
+++ b/src/windows-emulator/syscalls/file.cpp
@@ -174,6 +174,16 @@ namespace sogen
                     return STATUS_SUCCESS;
                 }
 
+                // The null device has no position, size or attributes to keep: it takes whatever is set.
+                if (file_handle == NUL_HANDLE)
+                {
+                    if (io_status_block)
+                    {
+                        io_status_block.write({});
+                    }
+                    return STATUS_SUCCESS;
+                }
+
                 return STATUS_INVALID_HANDLE;
             }
 
